{"study_accession":"PRJDB14085","secondary_study_accession":"DRP009745","title":"Human immune and gut microbial parameters associated with inter-individual variations in BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine-induced adaptive immunity","description":"COVID-19 mRNA vaccines induce protective adaptive immunity against SARS-CoV-2 in most individuals, but there is wide variation in levels of vaccine-induced antibody and T-cell responses. However, factors associated with this inter-individual variation are not fully understood. Here, using a systems biology approach based on multi-omics analyses of human blood and stool samples, we identify specific immune cell populations, genes, and gut microbes as novel correlates of Pfizer BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine-induced adaptive immunity. We find that a transcription module related to the AP-1 transcription factor network mediated by FOS, FOSB, and ATF3 is associated with low T-cell responses induced by BNT162b2 vaccination. FOS expression is associated with transcription modules related to baseline immunity, but it is negatively associated with those related to T-cell activation upon BNT162b2 mRNA stimulation. Interestingly, the gut microbial fucose/rhamnose degradation pathway is positively correlated with expression of FOS and ATF3, and inversely correlated with BNT162b2-induced T-cell responses. Taken together, these results demonstrate that baseline expression of AP-1 genes, which is associated with the gut microbial fucose/rhamnose degradation pathway, is a key negative correlate of BNT162b2-induced T-cell responses.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":11,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMD00517458","study_accession":"PRJDB14085","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"scRNA_Before_stim","description":"scRNA_Before_stim","sex":"pooled male and female","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-03-24","attributes":[["collection_date","missing"],["INSDC secondary accession","DRS285961"],["NCBI subm